our  our accurate guesses on empiric  antibiotic therapy so it's so important  to not only start antibiotics early but  really do it thoughtfully because if  we're not doing it thoughtfully we could  be worsening amr and we aren't  decreasing that patient's risk of  mortality  so of course your risk of getting it  wrong increases with amr as that data  shows  but how can we fix this how can we get  these as correct as possible well  straight off the bat with empiric  therapy we're never going to get it  perfect 100 but it's super important to  make sure we're diving into the  patient's past medical history past  cultures and trying to inform those  antibiotic decisions and then  fortunately the point of of our slides  today  and i'm sorry let me make sure i'm  pushing this to you  sorry so i'm so i was saying how can we  fix this and get 100 of our imperial  antibiotic  decisions correct  and so besides diving into their past  medical histories we have tools and we  have tools that we can use in a more  optimal way and i want to encourage us  bringing more and more attention to the  quality and use of our diagnostics  this will take some collaboration with  our pre-analytical teams but also our  decision-making teams on the back end  once these results are reported so if  pharmacy or the id teams can respond  more quickly to the initial gram scene  we are  impacting potentially mortality if the  teams can respond  quickly to that first id off let's say  they use multi  we are impacting mortality and then of  course  being able to have full confidence in  our antimicrobial susceptibility tests  to ensure the most accurate and narrow  therapy  we are possibly impacting mortality in  these sepsis patients so in summary i  just thought it was really helpful to  break down these guidelines and bring  our patients to the forefront of this  conversation and the importance of  diagnostics we should always be trying  to implement new performance improvement  programs  around