aring your  questions and we'll get started now  so first question for you dr murray we'd  love to pick your brain on  you mentioned the variability that can  exist in positivity rates we obtained  from blood cultures as well as  contamination rates so do you have  advice on how we can assess the  significance of a positive blood culture  so it's an interesting question um  and maybe because of my age i can cite  literature that's probably  published before some of you were born  but in the 1990s there are some really  very nice studies that i think are still  relevant today and they they were  performed by  mel weinstein arthraler and and a group  uh and what they did it was two separate  studies and each one looked  at  500 patients with positive blood  cultures  and then they made a clinical assessment  of the significance of the positive  isolates  and probably it shouldn't be a surprise  but  if if you looked at the recovery of  staph aureus in a blood culture about 75  percent of those patients turned out to  have significant uh isolates  and at that time and again remember this  is in the 1990s uh less than 10 percent  of the patients with coagulase negative  staphylococci  had significant cultures i think that's  changed dramatically  in our hospitals today with  use of  iv lines and  prosthetic devices such as heart valves  and so forth so the percent  significant quantity is negative  bacteremias is probably higher than what  it was in that study  if you look the gram-negative bacteria  the  e coli klebsiella all the gram negatives  almost all of those were significant  except for some of the non-fermenters  some  pseudomonas species other than the  rigidosa  but in general if it's a gram-negative  it's going to be clinically significant  the same was true for yeast infections  if you isolate yeast and blood that's  almost always clinically significant so  i think we can use that as a general  guideline  but it's always a clinical assessment  when you look at the patient and you  have to make the physician who's taking  care of that patient has to balance that  with the  isolate that you have  i think in the microbiology laboratory  because we have the ability to  identify organisms much better than what  we could historically  moving from biochemical testing to  the use of  maldi-tof  we're able to identify organisms that  many times the physician hasn't heard of  before and so one of our  responsibilities  is to understand the significance of  that organism it's its association with  infections and be able to  educate our physicians  about that so  again a challenge for the laboratories  now that we can identify organisms  maybe a  more sophisticated way than what we've  done historically  absolutely and another opportunity to  collaborate between the lab and the and  the decision making team to ensure  it's a very informed decision so thank  you for that break it breakdown  we talked about the importance of  minimizing turnaround times and ensuring  both accuracy but also efficiency for  cultures so  where have you seen in your in your  patient care experience the most  significant delays in being able to  relay positive results to the treatment  team  you know from from a microbiology  laboratory perspective we've always  focused on how fast does the organism  grow in the incubator  and we  try to understand the significance of  one hour faster growth and one type of  culture system versus another  and i think that's important to be aware  of that but that's not where the  problems are  the problems start in  taking that posit i'm sorry taking that  blood culture that's collected on the  floor  and getting it into the laboratory  and the studies that have looked at this  have shown that there are delays up to  15 hours particularly in emergency  departments and in highly busy areas  like icu's those cultures may be  collected but if they sit there in the  nurses station and don't get delivered  to the laboratory  we have a significant problem even under  root routine conditions there's  typically a three four five hour delay  before the coaches get there and if  they're collected in the evening it may  be a number of hours before it's  actually put into the incubator  the other area where i see significant  delays is  the blood culture signals positive in  your instrument  but it's not taken out of the instrument  and worked up  many times the laboratory don't  prioritize that positive blood cultures  now in some cases it's because  a significant proportion become positive  when nobody's in the laboratory  you know and that's something that  should be addressed for those  laboratories how can they prioritize  that positive that comes up at three in  the morning  and in other cases they prioritize that  we will look at and work up that  positive blood cultures at certain  periods during the day they don't do it  when it becomes positive but there may  be a delay of three four five six hours  before it's worked out  so if we really want to reduce  the amount of time it takes we have to  focus on first understanding where the  delays are in getting the bottles into  the laboratory and then where are the  delays in  the laboratory prioritizing the workup  and reporting  of those positive cultures  i think if we master that then we can  look at  i think what much less  significant delays such as how long does  it take for that bottle to grow in the  incubator  great thank you so much for that  breakdown  uh we got another question here  um that is how should we collect  cultures when we have an open system  such as syringes should we change  syringes between putting blood first  into the anaerobic bottle and then into  the aerobic bottle do you have advice on  this  um  so there are studies that have looked at  the value of changing the needle so and  some some  thoughts were that  the needle may be contaminated so remove  the needle replace it with a sterile  needle and then in