ort term  we all hope  that there'll be a molecular tool that  will allow very rapid direct detection  of organisms in the bloodstream  the challenges that we all recognize is  that there's very few organisms  circulating in the blood so you need  some way of concentrating those  organisms in order to optimize  your positive test result  um  you know i worked  for 10 plus years with beckham dickinson  we had we worked in this issue  every day  and i think we we all appreciated the  fact that it's a real challenge and i  would say anybody  you know in  my capacity and other companies would  also admit the same thing so  um  our hope is that we'll have a new  technology  um i'm a bit pessimistic i don't think  we're gonna see it  uh  in the near future near future five to  ten years i think there are  culture techniques like t2 the example  that you gave  is a complementary  tool  i don't think it will replace the blood  cultures at least currently not yet  great thank you so much for that input  one more question here i think we have  we'll we'll keep tr  jugging through these um for as long as  we can so one more is should we use peas  bottles only for adults patients who we  can't draw optimal blood volumes from  yeah i think i think it's a challenge if  you introduce pediatric bottles and say  it can be used for those  particular adult patients  my experience is that they become the  standard of care  and that really  goes against what what i talked about  before the importance of volume  it is interesting we  we did a series of studies uh internally  where we  looked at  what happens if you under fill a bottle  um  and it it turns out that there's not a  dramatic impact on that  so i would still use the  uh the adult size bottles for adults  even if you don't  add the full eight or ten mls because we  recognize certain patients you can't do  that their veins collapse they're  they may be adults but  you know they weigh less than 100 pounds  it's going to be very 